# Boring-side monomorphization (V1 extension): a generic struct (`Box<T>`)
# that ALSO has a concrete turbofish construction site elsewhere in this file
# (`Box<int>(...)`), combined with a method (`describe<U>`) that introduces
# its OWN separate type param `U` -- distinct from the struct's own `T` --
# called via its own turbofish (`b.describe<string>(...)`). Verifies both
# struct-level and method-level specialization compose: the specialized
# `Box_int` struct clone must carry the specialized `describe_string` method
# clone too (appended to the original generic `Box<T>` BEFORE the struct-level
# clone is made, so the clone-and-substitute machinery carries it along).
#
# `describe<U>` deliberately just passes `extra` straight through (no string
# interpolation of a T/U-typed value) -- interpolating a still-generic type
# param needs its own `Display` bound the transpiler doesn't infer today (a
# pre-existing, unrelated gap), which would make the still-generic `Box<T>`
# copy fail to compile even though nothing calls it directly in this file.
struct Box<T>:
    var T value

    def U describe<U>(U extra):
        extra

mut b = Box<int>(5)
print b.value
print b.describe<string>("hello")
